<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Massu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Massu (1908–2002) was a French general who had served with the Free French in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Commanding the 10th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">Parachute</a> Division, he was given police powers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1957 and broke the FLN network in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-algiers">Battle of Algiers</a> through mass arrests and torture. His role in the crisis of May 1958 helped bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> back to power, but his criticism of de Gaulle's Algerian policy led to his recall in 1960.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-massu.jpg" alt="Jacques Massu" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parachutistengeneraal_Massu,_Bestanddeelnr_909-5642.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Massu was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalons-en-champagne">Châlons-sur-Marne</a> on 5 May 1908 into a family of military officers; his father was an artillery officer. He graduated from Saint-Cyr in 1930 and chose the Colonial Infantry, serving with the 16th Senegalese Tirailleur Regiment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cahors">Cahors</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where he was cited for fighting around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a>, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> and from 1938 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, commanding the Tibesti subdivision. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> began he joined the Free French, fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> with General Leclerc and won the US Silver Star with the 2nd Armored Division at the Battle of Dompaire in September 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In October 1945 his mechanised Groupement Massu landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and was sent at once to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-tho">Mỹ Tho</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>. In 1956 his 10th Parachute Division took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a>, landing at Raswa and fighting at Port Said and Port Fuad, where French paratroopers often executed Egyptian prisoners.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> his forces identified and arrested the FLN leadership in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1957 by coercive interrogation and torture of members of lower cells. Massu was said to admit the use of torture willingly and insisted he would subject no one to treatment he had not first tried on himself. When right-wing elements seized power in Algiers on 13 May 1958, he became chairman of the Public Safety Committee and one of the leaders of the revolt, which threatened an assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> unless de Gaulle took charge. On 14 January 1960 he told the Süddeutsche Zeitung that the army had the power and would use it; summoned to Paris, he was relieved of command.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Massu later became military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and in 1966 général d'armée and chief of French forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On 29 May 1968 de Gaulle visited him at Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> during the events of May 68; according to some sources Massu made his support conditional on an amnesty for soldiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-armee-secrete">Organisation armée secrète</a>. He retired in July 1969. In 2000 the former FLN member Louisette Ighilahriz said he had been present when she was tortured and raped in 1957; Massu denied it but expressed regret that the paratroopers had used torture and rape. He died at Conflans-sur-Loing on 26 October 2002, aged 94.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacques Massu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war escalated after the Philippeville massacre of August 1955, and in 1957 paratroopers under General <b>Jacques Massu</b> broke the FLN network in the Battle of Algiers through the systematic use of torture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalons-en-champagne">Châlons-en-Champagne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Conflans-sur-Loing, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.96,4.36&amp;year=1969" aria-label="Châlons-en-Champagne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4651 455 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacques Massu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5101 725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5101 725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.96,4.36&amp;year=1969">What was here in 1969? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
